#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064979} :activity/id #uuid "8ea4ae18-ffc0-59bc-9ddc-fdba78903ea0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835 :topic/id #uuid "c490676a-a400-5088-9414-891ef037ec40" :topic/math-academy-id 296} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065394 :tutorial/content "Derivatives naturally occur in many real-world situations, so it's essential to know their units of measurement. The key is to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}?$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ measures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a148fb4-165b-500a-b7e6-a3c9c2776ae8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "31d20aff-dfc8-5c40-ac4f-e90082ea3b22" :step/math-academy-id 1040 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064152 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064035 :question/id #uuid "b7051104-6e23-5ab2-8779-5d54a3c3f5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-270"} :knowledge-point/id 